From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] EDEN_UP_MEM_CENSUS=1: who holds the memory, every 2 s while the pool's largest free block is under EDEN_UP_MEM_CENSUS_MIB (768) and at each area change, with the one change that frees the most; off by default

Not a port. The memory lines we have answer this only in pieces: EDEN_MEMORY, EDEN_POOL_LEDGER,
EDEN_VMA_USAGE and RADV_PS5_MEMORY come every 20 s, the guest memory report every 5 s under
256 MiB, and nothing splits the graphics caches by kind. In the t87-a door runs (run739) the
pool's largest block went from 5126 MiB (t=81 s) to 612 MiB (t=89 s) and 212 MiB (t=101 s); the
20 s summaries caught only the 612. This census reads the same counters at the moments that
matter and puts them in three lines (stderr.log), so the census just before a refusal or crash
says who held what.

When: a thread of its own ("MemCensus", placed by name like every other worker) asks the front for
the pool's largest free block every 250 ms (the call RefreshFreeMemory makes at 10 Hz). Under the
mark: a census every 2 s. An area load (AreaEvict's burst rule: EDEN_NX2_AREA_EVICT_BURST, 100,
new images within 30 frames, counted here on its own so EDEN_NX2_AREA_EVICT is not needed): a
census at once and one 3 s later. The first only marks the start; the second shows what the load
added.

  EDEN_MEM_CENSUS_AREA n frame new_images
  EDEN_MEM_CENSUS n why=low|area|area+3s t_s largest low (lowest since the last census) mark
      gpu=fresh|stale/<age>ms images=<MiB>/<count> sparse target astc other scaled stale_clean
      stale_written retiring decode_in_flight decode_kept copy_buffers msaa staging device upload
      download idle stream buffers async_ready/<waiting> cpu_scratch
  EDEN_MEM_CENSUS_POOL n out guest reserve jit tables heap (pieces live free_pages idle_arenas
      fragmented large in_use) vma_blocks vma_used vma_slack rest
  EDEN_MEM_CENSUS_TOP n short_by: 1 <name>=<MiB> 2 ... (up to 6); grew_most=<owner>+<MiB> since n-1

All in MiB. The first line splits what the allocator's blocks hold (images by the collector's own
measure: sparse = unbacked, target = GPU-written, astc, other; stale = in the LRU unused for 120
frames, clean or needing a copy back; retiring = deleted, memory not yet free; decode = the ASTC
decode images, in flight or kept; the staging pool's kept buffers, idle = the GPU is done and
nothing pins them; the buffer cache's buffers). async_ready and cpu_scratch are heap memory. The
POOL line splits everything out of the pool: guest memory, its reserve, JIT, tables, heap
(pieces = committed, live = blocks in use in them, free_pages = whole 2 MiB pages holding no block,
fragmented = the rest of the pieces, large = blocks of their own), the allocator's blocks, and
rest = out minus all of those: RADV's own buffers (RADV_PS5_MEMORY driver_mib less app), packed
guest slots (EDEN_SWAP_PACK held), Fine slots' blocks and whatever nobody counts. The two lines
overlap (the first is inside vma_used and heap): do not add them up.

Which single change frees the most: the first entry of EDEN_MEM_CENSUS_TOP, read at the last
census before the refusal or crash (or at area+3s of the failing transition). Each entry is what
one change could give back now:
  vma_slack        blocks less use: deleted images leave holes in shared blocks. Only whole blocks
                   go back: smaller blocks or dedicated memory for large images, or defragmenting
  stale_clean      EDEN_NX2_AREA_EVICT (a shorter age), or the collector
  stale_written    the same after a copy back (EDEN_NX2_AREA_EVICT_DIRTY drops them uncopied)
  heap_free_pages  a heap that gives whole free 2 MiB pages back (the trim returns idle arenas)
  heap_fragmented  only compaction or fewer long-lived blocks
  decode_kept      EDEN_NX2_TRANSCODE_KEEP_MIB=0
  decode_in_flight EDEN_PE_TRANSCODE_BOUND=1 with EDEN_PS5_TRANSCODE_PENDING_MIB
  staging_idle     EDEN_UP_STAGING_STEPS=1 (0506)
  copy_buffers     EDEN_NX2_TC_TEMP_BUFFER_FRAMES
  cpu_scratch      EDEN_TC_SCRATCH_KEEP_MIB
  async_ready      a lower EDEN_NX2_TC_KEEP_ASYNC
  retiring         nothing: it frees itself within 8 frames (what a GPU wait would release)
  guest_reserve    the guest's reserve (a refused graphics allocation already borrows it,
                   eden_guest_reserve_lend); a smaller EDEN_GUEST_RESERVE_FLOOR
grew_most names the owner that grew most since the previous census (images, decode, staging,
buffers, vma_slack, guest, heap, jit, rest, cpu_side). If it is not one of the entries above (guest,
jit, images in use), the fix is a budget for that owner, not a release. A freed byte helps the next
graphics allocation only if it comes back where that allocation fits: largest is one block, heap
pages come back 2 MiB at a time, and slack inside a VMA block only when the whole block empties.
From run739's own lines at t=89 s (largest 612 MiB): VMA blocks 2931 MiB with 2247 in use, so
vma_slack 684; heap free pages 94, fragmented ~249; between t=69 and t=89 the VMA blocks grew by
2476 MiB and guest memory by 1244 MiB.

Detail: the census 3 s after an area change and the first one under the mark in 10 s also call the
existing reports: RADV_PS5_MEMORY (prints with RADV_PS5_MEMORY_REPORT=1) and the guest memory
service's EDEN_CRAM / EDEN_SWAP / EDEN_SWAP_PACK lines, which carry the swapped-out and packed
split (the front has no cheap getter for those; the 2 s line leaves them in rest).

Cost, with the switch: per frame, four atomic loads (two in each cache) and the burst count (a few
integer operations), at the end of TextureCache::TickFrame and BufferCache::TickFrame. Per census, on the
GPU thread at its next frame end: one walk of the images and of the stale end of the LRU (as
EDEN_NX2_TC_LOG's 600-frame walk does), the decode images, copy buffers and kept staging buffers,
and the front's EDEN_VMA_USAGE function (the GPU thread owns the allocator). The census thread
waits at most 300 ms for these; a frame can take a second at a door, and then it prints gpu=stale
with the age of the last answer. The front's numbers are atomic reads (EDEN_POOL_LEDGER's
eden_direct_ledger, eden_heap_*, eden_guest_memory_reserve, Common::GuestMemoryUsage /
SparseUsage / CodeRangeUsage, eden_free_memory_bytes), declared weak and used only on ELF targets:
elsewhere the POOL line reads 0 and low censuses do not run (area censuses still do).
eden_free_memory_bytes is the front's function from 0503 (mig020 front); a front without it
(the T88 front) gets area censuses only, and the start line says "free memory not known here".
The first census only sets the baseline for grew_most once the GPU thread has answered once.

Not counted on their own: pipeline objects (host objects and RADV's compiler memory are heap
blocks, in live; compiled code is RADV's shader arenas, RADV_PS5_MEMORY shader=), JIT used against
held (EDEN_JIT_MEMORY per cache).

EDEN_UP_MEM_CENSUS (Common::PeSwitch, exactly "1"). EDEN_UP_MEM_CENSUS_MIB=<MiB> is a size, read
only with the switch on (unset, 0 or over 2^20: 768); on its own it does nothing. Unset: the
census thread, CensusTick and the buffer cache's answer never run; the texture cache carries four
more counters that stay 0; StagingBufferPool::CensusBytes and
TextureCacheRuntime::PublishMemoryCensus are only called by the census. Behaviour is as before.

Compared with PS5_Lichii's compactor (its src/guest_memory_service.inc and README, read 10 Nov),
what it gives back that ours does not:
- Packed slots share 2 MiB slabs, compacted in the background: when the slabs hold over 1.25x
  their copies plus 32 MiB, the copies in the emptiest slabs move to the open one and whole 2 MiB
  blocks go back. Ours (EDEN_GUEST_SWAP=3) gives each packed slot a block of 16 KiB pages of its
  own and never moves it, so packing turns the pool into small blocks (run739 at t=129 s: 496 MiB
  in 696 blocks under 2 MiB), a slot unpacked leaves a hole only another small block fits, and
  packing under pressure needs a new block before it frees one (reclaim stops at the first
  "no block for the packed bytes"). On the census this is in rest and in EDEN_SWAP_PACK held.
- Packing paced by pressure: under 2.5 GiB free it scans every 250 ms and packs what one pass
  left cold, up to 256 MiB a pass, before the area change asks. Ours packs slots untouched for
  EDEN_GUEST_SWAP_COLD_S (30 s) on a 2 s tick: at the door nothing cold is left (run739 t=129 s:
  untouched 30s=0MiB), so eden_guest_memory_reclaim (slots untouched for one tick) has little.
- A refused allocation of 64 MiB or more packs all it asked for there and then, taking any written
  slot when too few are cold; ours takes only slots armed and untouched for 2 s.
Already the same here: the heap in 32 MiB pieces, slots nobody wrote holding no memory. Its ASTC
conversion in 64 MiB layer batches is not part of the compactor; here it would show as
decode_in_flight.

PS5 port: every literal anchor tools/prepare-vulkan-port.py looks for in the touched files
(texture_cache.h, buffer_cache.h, vk_texture_cache.cpp, vk_staging_buffer_pool.h/.cpp; 31) still
matches as often as before, and no scheduler.Wait/Finish line is added (the port counts them).
Applies to T88 alone and after 0493-0510 (those that apply to T88).

